  <h2>What a scope of work is, and why it is the most valuable page in the contract</h2>
  <p>
    Every dispute about extra work is a dispute about scope. The drawings show what the building is;
    the scope of work says which parts of it you are building, for the price you quoted, and which
    parts you are not. It is the document a general contractor reads when your change order lands,
    the document a project manager reads when a curb is late and the crane is booked, and the
    document that becomes Exhibit A of the
    <a href="/templates/subcontractor-agreement/">subcontractor agreement</a> or the
    <a href="/templates/construction-contract/">construction contract</a>. A good one is short,
    specific, and organized the same way you bill.
  </p>
  <p>
    This template is built for the trade contractor writing its own scope — the version that goes
    out with the <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> and comes back attached
    to the contract. It also works as the scope an owner or GC issues to define a package for bidding,
    and as the scope for a remodel where the "drawings" are a sketch and a conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Included, excluded, assumed, by others: the four lists</h2>
  <p>
    The template is organized around four questions, each with its own section, because each settles
    a different kind of argument:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Included work</strong> settles "is this in your price?" One row per area or system, with
      the drawing sheets or spec section it comes from. Mirror your
      <a href="/templates/schedule-of-values/">schedule of values</a>: when the scope has twelve
      lines and the schedule of values has the same twelve, the pay application needs no
      interpretation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exclusions</strong> settle "who carries the adjacent work?" Most contracts say the work
      includes everything reasonably inferable except what is expressly excluded, so an exclusion you
      did not write down does not exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Assumptions and clarifications</strong> settle "what did the price depend on?" Access dates,
      working hours, one mobilization, lead times, the drawing revision you priced. When an assumption
      fails, this list is what turns the impact into a change order.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work by others</strong> settles "whose delay is it?" Each dependency gets a provider and
      a needed-by date. A missing pad or an unflashed curb is your problem until this table says otherwise.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Materials and submittals, milestones and acceptance criteria then say what you will submit and
    when, when the work happens, and what "done" means — so closeout is a checklist instead of a
    negotiation. The <a href="/blog/construction-project-closeout-checklist/">closeout checklist</a>
    is the other end of that thread.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is Summit Mechanical's scope for the Harbor Point Medical Office, Building
    B — the mechanical and plumbing subcontract used in every other sample on this site. Twelve
    included-work rows, from mobilization to closeout, each with drawing sheets and spec sections,
    in the same order as the $486,200 schedule of values. Ten exclusions, including power wiring to
    equipment, fire sprinkler, roofing at the RTU curbs, and furnishing the owner-supplied water
    heater. Six assumptions — underground released by June 8, a one-day crane set, RTU lead time of
    10–12 weeks. Six items by others with dates: curb flashing and the structural supports at RTU-2
    before the August 17 set, the water heater on site by July 15. Nine milestones from mobilization
    on June 1, 2026 to closeout on November 18, and acceptance criteria that name the TAB tolerance,
    the 48-hour controls trend and the inspections that must be signed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two of the job's change orders trace straight back to this document. Because the scope listed
    the water heater as furnished by the owner and installed by Summit, deleting it in July was a
    clean credit of the installation labor only (CO-002). Because the condensate drain from AHU-3
    was not on the M-402 the scope was based on, adding it after the drawing revision was a change,
    not an argument (CO-003). See the
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order template</a> for that one.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope of work vs. proposal vs. schedule of values</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Document</th><th>What it defines</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Scope of work</td>
        <td
          >The boundaries of the work: included, excluded, assumed, by others, milestones,
          acceptance</td
        >
        <td>Exhibit A to the contract</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">Bid proposal</a></td>
        <td
          >The offer: price, alternates, terms, validity — with the scope attached or summarized</td
        >
        <td>Sent before the contract; superseded by it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href="/templates/schedule-of-values/">Schedule of values</a></td>
        <td>The price broken into billable lines that sum to the contract amount</td>
        <td>Exhibit B to the contract; the basis of every pay application</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Read the guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-create-construction-bid/">creating a construction bid</a
    >
    for how the three fit together from takeoff to award.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this template is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Describing the work by trade name only — "HVAC per plans" — instead of by system with sheet
      and spec references.
    </li>
    <li>Skipping the exclusions because "everyone knows" who does the electrical.</li>
    <li>
      Assumptions that live in the estimator's head: the access date, the hours, the single
      mobilization the price was built on.
    </li>
    <li>Work by others with no needed-by date, so the delay has no owner.</li>
    <li>
      A scope that does not match the schedule of values, so every pay application starts with a
      reconciliation.
    </li>
    <li>
      No revision number, so three versions circulate and the contract attaches the wrong one.
    </li>
  </ul>
